## FDA recall lookup: naproxen lot `NOTREAL-77`

The FDA enforcement search returned **12 candidate naproxen recall records**. The supplied lot `NOTREAL-77` was **not found in the returned lot text**. The returned records include both ongoing and terminated recalls, with different formulations, manufacturers, NDCs, and distribution details. This was a bounded product-name search, not proof that every FDA record or manufacturer notice was reviewed.  
[ FDA/openFDA search results ](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22naproxen%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20)

### Can the nonmatch establish the bottle was unaffected?

**No.** A lot not appearing in this lookup does **not** establish that the bottle was unaffected or safe. FDA’s returned record specifically treats a lot nonmatch as **“not found in text—not clearance.”** The database may not include all notices or may describe affected products using ranges, “all lots” language, different lot formatting, or product/manufacturer identifiers.

### What still needs verification

Verify the bottle against the **specific official recall notice or the manufacturer/pharmacy**, including:

- exact product name and formulation—such as naproxen versus naproxen sodium, tablet versus suspension;
- manufacturer, repackager, or distributor;
- NDC or other product identifier;
- lot number exactly as printed, including punctuation or leading zeros;
- expiration date;
- whether the notice covers a lot range, date range, distribution period, or **all lots**;
- whether the product was obtained through a pharmacy, wholesaler, or other distribution channel covered by the notice.

The returned FDA records do not supply complete consumer instructions. The official FDA recall page is [FDA Drug Recalls](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/drug-recalls). The lookup itself cannot clear the bottle; confirmation should come from the manufacturer, dispensing pharmacy, or pharmacist using the complete package information. Changing or stopping a medication can carry risks and is a clinician/pharmacist conversation.

Informational only; not medical advice.

